  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nA weak shortwave ridge hanging over New Mexico will give way to a\nweak low hanging over Utah, pushing the deep subtropical moisture\nsouth of the border further into Mexico today. Surface winds will\nbe light and variable as scant cloud cover will keep \nprecipitation chances at zero.\n\nOnce the low reaches New Mexico, it will slightly expand on \nWednesday and marginally raise precipitation chances in the area \nmountains, though any rain that does fall will be minimal. Heading\ninto Thursday, the upper-level low moves deeper into West Texas \nas a weak Rex Block pattern forms over the Four Corners. The \nlowlands will stay dry, but moisture in the mountains will be \nsufficient enough to start a few showers/storms Thursday \nafternoon. Winds on Thursday will blow from the east at 8-12 mph.\n\nThe Rex Block is expected to weaken on Friday as the upper low moves \ndeeper into South Texas. With the exception of some spotty \nthunderstorms in the Gila on Saturday, conditions for Friday and\nSaturday will be mainly dry. On Sunday, both the GFS and ECMWF \nhave a Pacific trough moving into the western United States, \nincreasing rain chances for the western part of our region.\n\nSunday is also when both models begin to diverge. The latest GFS \nrun has the trough pull in what would be Simon, drenching southern\nCalifornia on Monday before advecting its moisture into our \nregion. The latest ECMWF run does not have Simon form in its \nlatest model run, but it still has moisture advected into our \nregion from the Gulf of California early next week. As of now, it \nis too soon to tell which track (if either) will win out, but we \nwill keep watch.",
